WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Film Courage: Why do you say that&nbsp;
most movie ideas are very simple?

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.440
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher and&nbsp;
Consultant: Well I think movies tend to be based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.760
on really simple stories. One of the things&nbsp;
that newer writers can end up doing without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.560
realizing it is over complicating their stories&nbsp;
right because they feel like especially if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.800
start writing without a solid outline and they&nbsp;
kind of get stuck they feel like oh I have to add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.640
more complications to give me more story to write&nbsp;
and they'll end up making things overly complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:34.960
where the movies that we tend to see are pretty&nbsp;
straightforward stories they're a concise setup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.040
some good escalations in the middle and then a&nbsp;
wrap it up in the end and it's the execution that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.560
makes things entertaining and interesting right&nbsp;
so it's it's not that we need very complex stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.480
in movies a lot of times overly complex stories&nbsp;
will be kind of hard to follow a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:59.600
bloated it'll be hard to kind of really go deep&nbsp;
in a character's experience in a story that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:05.520
too many complications or too many characters&nbsp;
uh whereas typically in movies what we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:12.160
is like one person pursuing one thing and what's&nbsp;
interesting or entertaining about it is the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:19.200
that that sort of is executed the interesting&nbsp;
cinematic cool stuff that happens along that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.360
journey which is actually a pretty simple journey&nbsp;
but it allows us the kind of space to dive into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.960
how that character is transforming the emotional&nbsp;
experience of it and kind of like the the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.800
cool cinematic stuff that happens along the&nbsp;
way does a simple idea mean a boring screenplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:45.360
no I i don't think so I think most if you look at&nbsp;
most movies it's actually a very simple storyline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.440
right it's a it's a pretty straightforward&nbsp;
one person trying to achieve one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.080
I think a lot of times newer writers maybe&nbsp;
worry that their stories are too simple and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:03.360
that will make them boring but if you have&nbsp;
a simple story that just gives you a a nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.800
sturdy framework to tell your story and then&nbsp;
it gives you the sort of space and the freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:15.280
to find the entertaining moments and cool stuff&nbsp;
in the execution of that story if that makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.960
right I know you've said and I don't know if it&nbsp;
was it was in the book or in an interview that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:27.040
cliche of sort of like the the pretty but doesn't&nbsp;
know it or the hot mess that sort of is rescued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:33.120
right that that's been done so much and I i agree&nbsp;
but what if somebody really needs to stick to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.360
the rom-com where high school reunion and best&nbsp;
friends getting married and does anybody object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.720
but they want to do the next memento or they want&nbsp;
to do the next crash something that takes a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.320
more putting together and you guide them and&nbsp;
say maybe you're not totally ready for that yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:58.400
yeah I think that that is a challenge right some&nbsp;
there are some stories that are I don't know if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.840
would call them more complex but the the execution&nbsp;
of them is more advanced right like crash is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.040
intersecting story lines kind of all put&nbsp;
together within the structure of a feature film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:17.040
I would call that like an sort of advanced craft&nbsp;
kind of movie right so I don't think that that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.720
uh necessarily an overly complicated story at&nbsp;
all and there definitely are newer writers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.760
who love movies like that and who want to&nbsp;
write something like that and that's what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

